**Current support.** Alpha HTML entries come from root activity: a non-trivial
root body, a const fragment or a runtime fragment. A general `$entry` purpose is
deferred; the earlier `$page` proposal is superseded and is not implemented.
Dependencies never activate their roots.

### Root activation

Top-level runtime code in a selected module root becomes a dormant entry. It
runs once when that module becomes a page entry. Helper files provide
declarations and never own an entry.

The entry has built-in `Error!`. Checked numeric failure and compatible top-level
postfix `!` can propagate to the generated terminal handler. Custom errors still
need explicit recovery. See @../progress (Progress) for current compiler support.

### Activity and future purpose

Any of these makes a normal root an HTML entry candidate: a non-trivial root
body, a direct const fragment or a runtime fragment. A direct named builder
contribution is a const fragment, so a metadata-only root still emits a complete
document:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[$in title:Welcome]
]

A nested or aliased insert remains ordinary template composition. Runtime named
builder slots are unsettled and are not supported.

A future general `$entry` purpose will make entry selection explicit. It is
deferred and is not current syntax. The earlier `$page` proposal is superseded.
`$doc`, `$note` and `$todo` templates are documentation/comment processing and
do not create an entry by themselves. A dependency cannot contribute its root
work or named slots to a consumer.

### Page fragments in brief

Direct top-level templates in the selected root remain fragments:

- A const fragment (the `#` prefix form) becomes static HTML.
- A runtime fragment (the plain bracket form) runs through the root's entry.

The builder keeps static and runtime fragments in source order. Assigned or
returned templates are ordinary values, and helpers cannot create page
fragments by themselves.

Runtime fragments publish only after that startup invocation succeeds. Failure
leaves static HTML in place and publishes none of the invocation's runtime
fragments; other side effects do not roll back. The generated terminal handler
shows a generic safe release notice rather than the application's `Error`
message as HTML. Development presentation belongs to the build/dev docs.
